16:["$","$L27",null,{"children":[[["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Nepali Date"}]," is based on the ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Bikram Sambat"}]," (Devanagari: ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"विक्रम संवत्"}],", abbreviated as ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"BS"}]," or ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"B.S."}],"), the official national civil calendar of ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Nepal"}],"."]}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Used across all organs of the Nepalese state—from parliamentary legislation and Supreme Court decrees to banking operations, taxation, school academic sessions, and day-to-day citizen records—Bikram Sambat is a sophisticated ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"solar sidereal calendar"}]," with roots extending over two millennia."]}],"\n",["$","hr",null,{}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h2","id":"historical-genesis-of-bikram-sambat","children":"Historical Genesis of Bikram Sambat"}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"the-founding-in-57-bce","children":"The Founding in 57 BCE"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Bikram Sambat originated in ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"57 BCE (Before Common Era)"}]," in the ancient city of ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Ujjain"}]," (in modern Madhya Pradesh, India). According to widespread historical tradition, the era was instituted by ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"King Vikramaditya of Ujjain"}]," to commemorate his decisive military victory over the invading Saka (Indo-Scythian) forces, liberating the region and relieving the financial debt of his citizens."]}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Because the era was inaugurated in 57 BCE, Bikram Sambat is approximately ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"56.7 years ahead"}]," of the Western Gregorian calendar (Common Era / AD)."]}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"evolution-of-the-eras-inscriptional-name","children":"Evolution of the Era's Inscriptional Name"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Epigraphic and numismatic records show that the name of this calendar era evolved across three distinct phases:"}],"\n",["$","ol",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Krita Era (कृत संवत्)"}],": Earliest stone inscriptions from the 3rd to 5th centuries CE referred to this system as ",["$","em",null,{"children":"Krita"}],", possibly honoring a foundational event or sovereign leader."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Malava Era (मालव संवत्)"}],": Between the 5th and 9th centuries CE, the era was called ",["$","em",null,{"children":"Malava Gana Sambat"}]," (the era of the Malava republic), corresponding to the migration and governance of the Malava tribe."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Vikrama Samvat (विक्रम संवत्)"}],": By the 9th and 10th centuries CE, starting prominently around the Dholpur stone inscription (dated 842 CE / 898 BS), the era became permanently identified with King Vikramaditya."]}],"\n"]}],"\n",["$","hr",null,{}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-calendar-eras-of-nepal","children":"The Calendar Eras of Nepal"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Nepal has one of the world's richest epigraphic and calendar traditions, having utilized several prominent eras throughout its documented history:"}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"1-saka-sambat-शक-संवत्---78-ce","children":"1. Saka Sambat (शक संवत् - 78 CE)"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Introduced in ancient Nepal during the Licchavi period, the Saka Sambat was the primary calendar of early royal inscriptions. The oldest dated stone epigraph in Nepal—the pillar inscription of ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"King Manadeva I at the Changu Narayan Temple"}],"—is dated ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"386 Saka Sambat (464 CE / 521 BS)"}],"."]}],"\n",["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"2-manadeva-sambat--amshuverma-era-576-ce","children":"2. Manadeva Sambat / Amshuverma Era (576 CE)"}],"\n","$L29","\n","$L2a","\n","$L2b","\n","$L2c","\n","$L2d","\n","$L2e","\n","$L2f","\n","$L30","\n","$L31","\n","$L32","\n","$L33","\n","$L34","\n","$L35","\n","$L36","\n","$L37","\n","$L38","\n","$L39","\n","$L3a","\n","$L3b","\n","$L3c","\n","$L3d","\n","$L3e","\n","$L3f","\n","$L40","\n","$L41","\n","$L42","\n","$L43","\n","$L44","\n","$L45","\n","$L46","\n","$L47","\n","$L48","\n","$L49","\n","$L4a","\n","$L4b","\n","$L4c","\n","$L4d","\n","$L4e","\n","$L4f","\n","$L50","\n","$L51","\n","$L52","\n","$L53","\n","$L54","\n","$L55","\n","$L56","\n","$L57","\n","$L58","\n","$L59","\n","$L5a","\n","$L5b","\n","$L5c","\n","$L5d","\n","$L5e","\n","$L5f","\n","$L60","\n","$L61","\n","$L62"],"$L63"]}]
