29:["$","p",null,{"children":"During the late Licchavi period under rulers such as King Amshuverma and King Jisnugupta, a shortened regional calendar system known as the Manadeva Sambat was employed across the Kathmandu Valley for public administration and religious foundations."}]
2a:["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"3-nepal-sambat-नेपाल-संवत्---879-ce--936-bs","children":"3. Nepal Sambat (नेपाल संवत् - 879 CE / 936 BS)"}]
2b:["$","p",null,{"children":["Founded by ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Shankhadhar Sakhwa"}]," on Kartik Shukla Pratipada in 879 CE after he cleared the debts of all citizens in the Kathmandu Valley during the reign of King Raghavadeva. Nepal Sambat served as the principal civic, lunar, and commercial calendar of the Malla Dynasty (1200–1768 CE) and remains revered today as a national heritage calendar of Nepal."]}]
2c:["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"4-bikram-sambat-in-ancient-and-medieval-nepal","children":"4. Bikram Sambat in Ancient and Medieval Nepal"}]
2d:["$","p",null,{"children":"While Nepal Sambat predominated in the Kathmandu Valley's domestic trade, Bikram Sambat maintained continuous usage across western and northern Nepal:"}]
2e:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":["In the ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Khasa Malla Empire"}]," of the Karnali basin (centered in the Sinja Valley, Jumla), Bikram Sambat was the undisputed official calendar. The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Dullu Stone Pillar of King Prithvi Malla"}]," (located in Dailekh) is engraved in Bikram Sambat ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"1414 BS (1357 CE)"}]," using the ancient Khas language."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"King Jayasthiti Malla (reigned 1382–1395 CE) and King Pratap Malla of Kathmandu utilized Bikram Sambat alongside Nepal Sambat in stone treaties, temple edicts, and Sanskrit panegyrics."}],"\n"]}]
2f:["$","$L28",null,{"as":"h3","id":"5-unification-and-official-adoption-in-nepal-17681901-ce","children":"5. Unification and Official Adoption in Nepal (1768–1901 CE)"}]
30:["$","p",null,{"children":["When ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"King Prithvi Narayan Shah of Gorkha"}]," unified Nepal and established Kathmandu as the capital in ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"1825 BS (1768 CE)"}],", he adopted Bikram Sambat as the standard calendar for royal seals (",["$","em",null,{"children":"Lal Mohar"}],"), military directives, treaties, and administrative governance."]}]
31:["$","p",null,{"children":["In ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"1958 BS (1901 CE)"}],", during the premiership of Rana Prime Minister ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Chandra Shumsher Jung Bahadur Rana"}],", Bikram Sambat was formally promulgated by imperial decree as the ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"sole official civil and administrative calendar of the Kingdom of Nepal"}],", standardizing astronomical solar calculations across all government offices."]}]
